</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Underwater simulation environment:</span> To simulate diverse underwater visual conditions with varying turbidity, we incorporate a physics-based underwater imaging formation model described in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>, which accounts for image degradation caused by absorption and backscattering, as well as forward scattering and noise induced by marine snow. The imaging formation model is formulated as follows:</p>
<table id="S6.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{c}(x)=(J_{c}\ast h_{f})(x)\cdot e^{-\beta_{c}z(x)}+B_{c}^{\infty}(1-e^{-\beta_{c}z(x)})+B_{c}^{\text{N}}(x)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>c</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>c</mi><mtext>N</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{c}(x)=(J_{c}\ast h_{f})(x)\cdot e^{-\beta_{c}z(x)}+B_{c}^{\infty}(1-e^{-\beta_{c}z(x)})+B_{c}^{\text{N}}(x),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{c}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{c}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the pixel intensity of the clear scene at pixel location <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> for color channel <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in\{R,G,B\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in\{R,G,B\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the distance from the camera to the scene point. The term <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is the depth-dependent point spread function (PSF), which models forward scattering as a Gaussian blur whose standard deviation is proportional to distance, specifically <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{h}\cdot z(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{h}\cdot z(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. The terms <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{c}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>c</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{c}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{c}^{\text{N}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>c</mi><mtext>N</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{c}^{\text{N}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> represent the background ambient light and the image noise caused by the marine snow and suspended particles, respectively. <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{c}^{\text{N}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>c</mi><mtext>N</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{c}^{\text{N}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is computed as follows:</p>
<table id="S6.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{c}^{\text{N}}(x)=k_{M}M(x)\left(1-B_{c}^{\infty}\right)\left(1-e^{-\beta_{c}z(x)}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>c</mi><mtext>N</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>c</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{c}^{\text{N}}(x)=k_{M}M(x)\left(1-B_{c}^{\infty}\right)\left(1-e^{-\beta_{c}z(x)}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(x)\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(x)\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is the Perlin noise map with a strength ratio denoted as <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. We simulate three water conditions (Type IC, 3C, 7C) from clear to turbid based on the Jerlov water types <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>. The corresponding parameters are summarized in <a href="#S6.T2" title="In VI-B1 Simulator Setting Up ‣ VI-B Simulation Experiments ‣ VI Experiments ‣ DUViN: Diffusion-Based Underwater Visual Navigation via Knowledge-Transferred Depth Features" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>, the visual examples are shown in <a href="#S6.F6" title="In VI-B1 Simulator Setting Up ‣ VI-B Simulation Experiments ‣ VI Experiments ‣ DUViN: Diffusion-Based Underwater Visual Navigation via Knowledge-Transferred Depth Features" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S6.F8.fig3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S6.F8.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.02983v1/test1.png" id="S6.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/268;" width="476" height="268" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S6.F8.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.02983v1/test1p.svg" id="S6.F8.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">In-air testing environment</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S6.F8.fig6" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S6.F8.fig4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.02983v1/test2.png" id="S6.F8.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/268;" width="476" height="268" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S6.F8.fig5" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.02983v1/test2p.svg" id="S6.F8.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Type IC testing environment</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 8</span>: </span><span id="S6.F8.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">NoMaD failure cases under in-air and underwater conditions.<span id="S6.F8.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Example observations across water types, highlighting the difficulty in degraded visibility.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AUV dynamics model:</span> To simulate the realistic behaviors of the AUV, we employ the 6-DoF dynamic model described in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>. The dynamics are formulated as follows:</p>
<table id="S6.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}\dot{\nu}+\mathcal{C}(\nu)\nu+\mathcal{D}(\nu)\nu+g(\eta)=\tau_{f}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ν</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}\dot{\nu}+\mathcal{C}(\nu)\nu+\mathcal{D}(\nu)\nu+g(\eta)=\tau_{f},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu\in\mathbb{R}^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu\in\mathbb{R}^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the AUV’s linear and angular velocities in the body frame. <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}\in\mathbb{R}^{6\times 6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}\in\mathbb{R}^{6\times 6}</annotation></semantics></math> is the inertia matrix including both rigid-body and added mass effects, <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\nu)\in\mathbb{R}^{6\times 6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\nu)\in\mathbb{R}^{6\times 6}</annotation></semantics></math> captures Coriolis and centripetal forces, and <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}(\nu)\in\mathbb{R}^{6\times 6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}(\nu)\in\mathbb{R}^{6\times 6}</annotation></semantics></math> models hydrodynamic damping. The term <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\eta)\in\mathbb{R}^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\eta)\in\mathbb{R}^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> represents restoring forces due to gravity and buoyancy. The torque input is denoted by <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{f}\in\mathbb{R}^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{f}\in\mathbb{R}^{6}</annotation></semantics></math>. The definitions of the above dynamic terms are detailed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, and the parameter values of the AUV model are based on the BlueROV2 Heavy configuration, as referenced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Given the generated velocity commands <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in 4 DoF, the remaining velocities in pitch and roll are regulated to zero. We employ a simple PD controller for tracking, with the input torque defined as:</p>
<table id="S6.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{f}=k_{p}e+k_{d}\dot{e}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{f}=k_{p}e+k_{d}\dot{e},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in\mathbb{R}^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in\mathbb{R}^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the velocity error, and <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{e}\in\mathbb{R}^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{e}\in\mathbb{R}^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> represents its time derivative. The proportional gain <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and the derivative gain <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> are both diagonal matrices in <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{6\times 6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{6\times 6}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S6.T3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TABLE III</span>: </span><span id="S6.T3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Obstacle avoidance performance under different water conditions at fixed altitude.<span id="S6.T3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> We evaluate the success rate (Succ.%), collision-free rate (CF%), and average number of collisions (A. C.) across varying water types. DUViN and DUViN-air are compared against NoMaD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. While NoMaD fails to navigate under any underwater condition, both DUViN and DUViN-air demonstrate strong robustness, with DUViN (with transferred encoder) maintaining 100% success and more robust performance as turbidity increases.</span></span></figcaption>
<div id="S6.T3.6" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:30.3pt;vertical-align:-13.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-182.4pt,16.0pt) scale(0.486040119540066,0.486040119540066) ;">
